1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,087
Your siding is the first thing people see.

2
00:00:03,937 --> 00:00:07,811
And in North Vancouver, it's also your home's first line of defence.

3
00:00:08,661 --> 00:00:11,812
Choose the wrong material, or install it the wrong way,

4
00:00:11,812 --> 00:00:13,950
and the rain will find every mistake.

5
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:18,001
In this guide, we'll walk through how siding works on the

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,147
North Shore, which materials hold up best, what a proper

7
00:00:21,147 --> 00:00:24,404
installation looks like, and how to know when it's time to

8
00:00:24,404 --> 00:00:25,232
replace yours.

9
00:00:26,082 --> 00:00:29,193
North Vancouver sits right against the mountains.

10
00:00:29,193 --> 00:00:32,677
Moist Pacific air hits the slopes and falls as rain, so

11
00:00:32,677 --> 00:00:36,099
the North Shore gets noticeably more rain than most of

12
00:00:36,099 --> 00:00:37,156
Metro Vancouver.

13
00:00:38,006 --> 00:00:41,551
Add long, grey winters, salt air near the water, and shade

14
00:00:41,551 --> 00:00:45,576
from tall evergreens, and you get walls that stay damp for months.

15
00:00:46,426 --> 00:00:50,137
That's why, here in coastal B.C., the building code calls

16
00:00:50,137 --> 00:00:52,825
for a rainscreen on new wood-frame walls:

17
00:00:52,825 --> 00:00:56,408
a small air gap behind the siding that lets water drain

18
00:00:56,408 --> 00:00:58,071
out and the wall dry out.

19
00:00:58,921 --> 00:01:01,144
Siding alone doesn't keep water out.

20
00:01:01,144 --> 00:01:02,765
The system behind it does.

21
00:01:03,615 --> 00:01:05,493
So which material should you choose?

22
00:01:05,493 --> 00:01:07,269
Let's go through the main options.

23
00:01:08,119 --> 00:01:11,372
Fibre cement is the most popular choice on the North

24
00:01:11,372 --> 00:01:14,871
Shore. It's rot-proof, it won't burn, and it holds paint

25
00:01:14,871 --> 00:01:18,125
well. It's heavier and needs careful cutting, but it

26
00:01:18,125 --> 00:01:20,273
handles our wet climate very well.

27
00:01:21,123 --> 00:01:22,804
Vinyl is the budget option.

28
00:01:22,804 --> 00:01:26,164
It's light and low-maintenance, but it can crack in the

29
00:01:26,164 --> 00:01:29,585
cold, fade in the sun, and it rarely matches the look of

30
00:01:29,585 --> 00:01:30,665
higher-end homes.

31
00:01:31,515 --> 00:01:34,887
Engineered wood gives you a real wood look at a lower

32
00:01:34,887 --> 00:01:38,259
cost. Just keep in mind that every cut edge has to be

33
00:01:38,259 --> 00:01:40,320
sealed, or moisture will get in.

34
00:01:41,170 --> 00:01:44,501
Natural cedar is beautiful, and a West Coast classic.

35
00:01:44,501 --> 00:01:48,140
But it needs regular staining or sealing, or it will grey,

36
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:49,127
split, and rot.

37
00:01:49,977 --> 00:01:53,601
Metal and composite panels give you the clean, modern look

38
00:01:53,601 --> 00:01:56,550
you see on new builds. They're durable and very

39
00:01:56,550 --> 00:01:59,928
low-maintenance, but the joints and corners have to be

40
00:01:59,928 --> 00:02:01,157
detailed perfectly.

41
00:02:02,007 --> 00:02:05,439
And for many modern homes, the best answer is a mix:

42
00:02:05,439 --> 00:02:09,713
brick or stucco in one area, and panels or lap siding in another.

43
00:02:10,563 --> 00:02:12,592
Here's what that looks like in real life.

44
00:02:13,442 --> 00:02:16,874
On this townhome project, warm brick is paired with dark

45
00:02:16,874 --> 00:02:18,801
lap siding on the upper floors.

46
00:02:18,801 --> 00:02:22,232
The black window boxes and trim tie it all together, and

47
00:02:22,232 --> 00:02:25,724
the darker top makes the building feel lighter and lower.

48
00:02:26,574 --> 00:02:30,534
On this duplex, smooth white stucco is combined with dark

49
00:02:30,534 --> 00:02:32,924
panels and warm wood-tone soffits.

50
00:02:32,924 --> 00:02:36,884
Clean lines, strong contrast, and every joint detailed to

51
00:02:36,884 --> 00:02:37,703
shed water.

52
00:02:38,553 --> 00:02:41,894
Whatever material you pick, the installation decides how

53
00:02:41,894 --> 00:02:44,238
long it lasts. Here's what to look for.

54
00:02:45,088 --> 00:02:47,538
A weather barrier lapped the right way.

55
00:02:47,538 --> 00:02:49,131
A drained rainscreen gap.

56
00:02:49,131 --> 00:02:51,826
Flashing above every window, door and deck.

57
00:02:51,826 --> 00:02:56,053
Sealed cut edges. And proper clearance from the ground and the roof.

58
00:02:56,903 --> 00:03:00,181
If a contractor can't explain these details, keep looking.

59
00:03:01,031 --> 00:03:04,229
So how do you know if your siding needs attention?

60
00:03:04,229 --> 00:03:07,929
Watch for soft or swollen boards, peeling paint that keeps

61
00:03:07,929 --> 00:03:11,628
coming back, mould or green staining, gaps and cracks, and

62
00:03:11,628 --> 00:03:13,447
stains on your inside walls.

63
00:03:14,297 --> 00:03:16,337
Small problems are cheap to fix.

64
00:03:16,337 --> 00:03:19,181
Hidden water damage behind the siding is not.

65
00:03:20,031 --> 00:03:21,930
To help any siding last longer:

66
00:03:21,930 --> 00:03:25,372
wash it once a year, keep your gutters clean, trim plants

67
00:03:25,372 --> 00:03:28,517
back from the walls, and recheck the caulking around

68
00:03:28,517 --> 00:03:30,357
windows every couple of years.

69
